<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="act-intro-relations">
  <title>Introduction to Relations</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      For the following, recall that if <m>R</m> is a relation, the statement <m>aRb</m> means that <m>a</m> is related to <m>b</m> under the relation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Come up with some relations (human, number, set, and other) apart from the ones discussed in the video.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For the relations <m>\le</m> (i.e. less than or equal), <m>\mid</m> (i.e. divisibility), and <m>\subseteq</m> (i.e. subset relation), define the relation formally as a set of ordered pairs, using set builder notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find 4 elements of each relation in #2 (i.e. 4 ordered pairs).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Define the hostility relationship <m>H</m> on the set <m>P</m> of all people where <m>aHb</m> means that <m>a</m> is mad at <m>b</m>. Let Jojo be a person.  Describe the following set, interpreting <m>H</m> as a set of ordered pairs, and using set builder notation:  The set of people who are mad at Jojo (poor Jojo!).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Do what you did in #3 for all the other relations we discussed in class.  Describe the sets informally, and then describe them formally using set builder notation.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>